.TH EUCLID-CLI-EDS-GET-ZONE 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 2.3.2" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eds-get-zone \- show a zone
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eds get-zone
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I ZONE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows a zone: its TTL, its description, its SOA serial and how many
record sets it holds. The count is counted when asked rather than kept,
so it is always right.
.PP
The serial is the time of the zone's last change, in seconds since the
epoch \- creating it, or writing or deleting any record in it. Comparing
it with what a DNS client sees in the SOA says whether the server has
picked the change up yet:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dig @127.0.0.1 -p 1053 corp.example SOA +short
.fi
.RE
.PP
A zone of another account is answered as not existing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fIZONE\fR
The zone's apex. Required.
.SH OUTPUT
The zone, as JSON:
.IR name ,
.IR ern ,
.IR namespace ,
.IR ttl ,
.IR description ,
.IR recordCount ,
.IR serial ,
.I created
and
.IR modified .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The zone was shown.
.TP
.B 1
No such zone in your account (HTTP 404), or a grant does not allow
reading it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eds get-zone --name corp.example
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eds (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eds-update-zone (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eds-list-zones (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eds-list-records (1)
